{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed807260302/8/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7b0280bc\" lines=\"54-69\">\n<description>\nRecolor the shortest path between two terminal squares in a square-and-track graph.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Two non-bg colors form solid NxN squares (N>=2): one color marks exactly 2 terminals, the other marks stations.\n- A third non-bg color forms 1-cell-wide track segments connecting pairs of squares (graph edges).\n\nRule:\n- Find the shortest path (fewest edges) between the two terminal squares.\n- Recolor every station square on that path to green.\n- Recolor every track segment along that path to grey.\n- Everything else unchanged.\n\nKey insight: The grid encodes a graph where squares are nodes and tracks are edges; the task is graph shortest-path, not spatial proximity.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"a644e277\" lines=\"31-50\">\n<description>\nExtract the lattice subregion bounded by the four background-colored corner holes.\n\nInput structure:\n- A regular one-cell-thick lattice has one line color, one background color,\n  and sparse marker pixels.\n- Exactly four lattice-line intersections are background-colored holes instead\n  of line-color pixels.\n\nRule:\n- Find the unique non-background color that forms regular horizontal and\n  vertical lattice lines.\n- Use the four background-colored lattice intersections as the corners of one\n  rectangle.\n- Return the inclusive crop between those corner rows and columns.\n\nKey insight:\n- The crop is marked by the missing lattice intersections, not by the marker\n  pixels inside the cells.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1a244afd\" lines=\"49-68\">\n<description>\nRotate each magenta source pixel 90 degrees counterclockwise around its matched blue pivot, recoloring the new pixel orange.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- The only foreground colors are blue pivot pixels and magenta source pixels.\n- Each magenta source can be paired one-to-one with a blue pivot in the same\n  row or column.\n\nRule:\n- Remove all magenta source pixels.\n- For each matched source/pivot pair, preserve the source distance from the\n  pivot but rotate its offset 90 degrees counterclockwise.\n- Paint the rotated in-bounds target orange; if the target is outside the\n  grid, the source simply disappears.\n\nKey insight:\nThe blue pixels are rotation centers, and row/column alignment gives a\nunique global matching from each magenta source to its pivot.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8618d23e\" lines=\"9-25\">\n<description>\nStagger the two horizontal input regions onto a larger maroon separator canvas.\n\nInput structure:\n- The input is a rectangular grid with an even number of rows.\n- The top half and bottom half are the two regions to preserve.\n- Maroon is absent from the input and is used only as the new separator color.\n\nRule:\n- Create an output with one extra row and one extra column, filled with maroon.\n- Copy the top half into the upper-left corner.\n- Copy the bottom half one row below the top region and one column to the right.\n\nKey insight:\nThe output is not a recoloring of either region; it inserts an L-shaped maroon\ngap by diagonally offsetting the lower half from the upper half.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9b5080bb\" lines=\"161-172\">\n<description>\nRecolor the small defect bars on each shared-color object with the opposite panel color.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid contains two large panel colors and one shared object color.\n- Each shared-color object is a mostly solid rectangle inside exactly one panel.\nRule\n- Find the near-solid rectangular core of each object.\n- Missing core pixels and one-cell protrusions define two-pixel bars perpendicular to the nearest core edge.\n- Recolor those bars with the other panel color; leave all other pixels unchanged.\nKey insight\n- The colors to add are not tied to numeric ids: each object's surrounding panel identifies the opposite panel color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"985ae207\" lines=\"82-97\">\n<description>\nRepeat each 3x3 control-center sprite toward the matching solid rectangle until a copy first overlaps that rectangle.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Each sprite is a 3x3 foreground object whose eight border pixels have one color and whose center has a different control color.\n- For every control color, there is exactly one separate solid rectangular object of that color.\n\nRule\n- Match each sprite to the unique solid rectangle whose color equals the sprite center.\n- Copy the whole 3x3 sprite in the cardinal direction of that rectangle, stepping by three cells each time.\n- Stop after pasting the first copy whose 3x3 bbox overlaps the rectangle; copied pixels overwrite the rectangle there.\n\nKey insight\nThe center pixel is not the paint color: it is a destination key, while the entire 3x3 sprite is the motif tiled along the straight path.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"9aaea919\" lines=\"38-51\">\n<description>\nTransfer sprites from red-marked lanes to green-marked lanes, recoloring and stacking.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Red (2) and green (3) thin bars define vertical lanes.\n- Each lane contains repeated identical monochrome sprites in an evenly spaced vertical grid.\n\nRule:\n- Each red lane's sprites turn grey (5); their count transfers to the nearest green lane.\n- New sprites fill empty lattice slots bottom-up, using the source shape recolored to the destination lane's sprite color.\n- All marker bars are removed.\n\nKey insight: Sprite count is conserved across lanes — red sprites become grey, and equally many new sprites stack into the green lane's regular grid.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"80a900e0\" lines=\"69-82\">\n<description>\nExtend diagonal rays outward from outer-border segments adjacent to diamond shapes on a checkerboard.\n\nInput structure:\n- Checkerboard bg (two most common colors).\n- Hollow diamond outlines (8-pixel) in a non-bg color (inner border).\n- Short diagonal line segments in another non-bg color adjacent to each diamond edge (outer border).\n\nRule:\n- For each outer-border segment, determine the outward direction (perpendicular to the segment, away from the diamond).\n- From each endpoint of the segment, cast a diagonal ray outward, painting over bg pixels with the segment's color until hitting a non-bg pixel or the grid edge.\n\nKey insight: Rays emit only from the two endpoints of each outer-border segment, not every pixel, and travel diagonally away from the diamond.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8e5c0c38\" lines=\"9-21\">\n<description>\nClean each color layer by enforcing vertical mirror symmetry.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Several non-bg colors, each forming a nearly-symmetric pattern about some vertical axis.\n- Some pixels in each color are \"noise\" that breaks the symmetry.\n\nRule:\n- For each non-bg color, find the vertical axis that best explains its pixel layout.\n- Remove any pixel that lacks a same-color mirror counterpart across that axis.\n\nKey insight: Each color has its own independent symmetry axis; the task is per-color denoising, not whole-grid symmetry.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"aaef0977\" lines=\"25-41\">\n<description>\nReplace an orange canvas with concentric Manhattan-distance color rings centered on the lone marker pixel.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is an orange background with exactly one non-orange marker pixel.\n- The marker color is one of the colors in the fixed non-orange ring cycle.\n\nRule:\n- The marker location is distance 0 and keeps the marker color.\n- Each step of Manhattan distance away from the marker advances one position\n  through the cycle green, yellow, black, grey, red, teal, maroon, pink, blue.\n- Fill every pixel of the original grid by this distance-indexed cycle.\n\nKey insight: The output is not copied from existing objects; it is the\ndistance field around the single marker, colored by a cyclic palette that\nexcludes the orange background.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"458e3a53\" lines=\"19-32\">\n<description>\nExtract the compact table of solid cells hidden inside a separator lattice.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has full-width and full-height bars of one separator color, dividing it into rectangular cells.\n- Most cells contain a repeating multicolor pattern, while a contiguous rectangle of cells is solid monochrome.\n\nRule\n- Split the input into lattice cells using the full separator rows and columns.\n- Keep the cells whose full interior is one color, crop to their occupied rectangle, and output those colors.\n\nKey insight\n- The periodic pattern is noise; only completely solid lattice cells encode the reduced output grid.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"df9fd884\" lines=\"33-47\">\n<description>\nMove the colored object from the lower bracket lane to the upper bracket lane and drop it to the bottom edge.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- One frame color forms exactly two 4-connected bracket objects: a top-edge bracket open downward and a bottom-edge bracket open upward.\n- One other 4-connected foreground object sits in the lower bracket's interior column span.\n\nRule\n- Remove the movable object from its original cells.\n- Keep its shape and color unchanged.\n- Paste it with its bbox columns aligned to the top bracket's interior columns and with its bbox bottom aligned to the grid bottom.\n\nKey insight\n- The lower bracket identifies the object's source lane; the upper bracket provides the target lane, and the final vertical placement is bottom-aligned.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"652646ff\" lines=\"84-105\">\n<description>\nRecover each partially hidden 6x6 ring sprite and stack complete copies from top layer to bottom layer.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- Several non-background colors are visible fragments of the same 12-pixel\n  outline ring in a 6x6 box; small off-template marker pixels are noise.\n- Ring fragments overlap, so upper rings replace some expected pixels of\n  lower rings, and a ring may also be clipped by the input boundary.\n\nRule:\n- Identify every substantial color whose pixels all lie on one translated\n  copy of the ring template.\n- For each missing in-bounds template pixel, the color currently occupying\n  that pixel is above the hidden ring.\n- Topologically sort these occlusion relations from top to bottom, then draw\n  one complete 6x6 ring for each color in that order on a background canvas.\n\nKey insight:\n- The output is not a crop of the scene; it is the layer stack of complete\n  ring sprites reconstructed from their visible fragments.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"agent_message","text":"实现方案分三块：\n\n1. 识别逻辑  \n   - 扫描所有非背景颜色，找到“几乎完整的单像素矩形边框”组件，把恰好缺 1 个边框像素的矩形认作围栏。  \n   - 从围栏外扩一圈定位唯一缺口方向；从内区提取唯一前景颜色及其 4 连通形状。  \n   - 单独识别模板区：由 `grey` 模板块和 `maroon` 分隔列/行组成，读出每个模板的局部形状并建立旋转归一化索引。\n\n2. 变换规则  \n   - 按内部形状的旋转等价类匹配模板。  \n   - 盖印时不改变模板区，只在缺口外侧贴一个与模板同尺寸的彩色副本；朝向按缺口方向旋转，使模板从缺口朝外延伸。  \n   - 为了让规则自洽且可学，生成器会固定“模板原始朝向 = 朝上缺口时的盖印朝向”，其余方向由 90 度旋转得到。\n\n3. 生成器与验证  \n   - 随机模板形状、围栏数量、围栏尺寸、缺口方向、颜色、模板区位置。  \n   - 强约束避免重叠、越界、歧义匹配，并保证四个方向和多种模板长期都会出现。  \n   - 写入目标文件后跑 100 次自检和 `py_compile`，最后删掉 `__pycache__`。"}}
